# Literal modifiers are uniformly adjacent identifier-plus-literal syntax. A
# modifier names a compile-time handler; it is not a runtime call or a value
# binding. The ordinary identifier and literal tokens are paired by this
# production, and @nospace requires them to be adjacent.
# Resolution uses compile-time literal-handler and type metadata.
# The prefix must be adjacent to its payload. Examples:
#
#     set{1, 2, 3}
#     regex"[a-z]+"
#     ffi[- int puts(const char *); -]
#     image[- ... -]
#
# A parameterized prefix may carry type information, as in set[int]{}. Integer
# and floating-point literals do not permit modifiers; the other permitted
# literal shapes are selected by their registered handler.
# There is no postfix literal-modifier syntax.
#
# The handler runs after lexical decoding and before type checking completes.
# It receives the prefix arguments, decoded payload, raw host-source span, and the
# payload form. It returns a typed compile-time value or syntax object. Failure
# is a compile-time diagnostic anchored in the literal; there is no runtime
# fallback. Cached results must include the handler and all declared external
# inputs in their cache key.
<tagged-lit> @notrivia($1) @nospace($1) @adjacent("prefix") @infer("`t") @emit("tagged_literal") ::=
